.SH NAME
perlos390 \- building and installing Perl for z/OS (previously called OS/390)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
This document will help you Configure, build, test and install Perl
on z/OS Unix System Services.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
Perl 5.44 on z/OS has been tested on z/OS 3.1 using "Unix System Services".
There have been extensive changes in the build process (such as using a
different compiler) from earlier releases, which were tested on earlier
versions of z/OS.  It could well be that this release of Perl will work on
different z/OS versions.
.SS "EBCDIC vs ASCII"
.IX Subsection "EBCDIC vs ASCII"
The native character set for z/OS is EBCDIC (code page 1047 in North America),
but recent z/OS releases have added the ability for it to handle ASCII as well.
And an open source project now exists to port many open source tools, such as
\&\f(CW\*(C`git\*(C'\fR, and yes \f(CW\*(C`Perl\*(C'\fR to take advantage of these new capabilities.  (A blog
post about its early development is here
<https://makingdeveloperslivesbetter.wordpress.com/2022/01/18/enabling\-open\-source\-for\-z\-os\-perl/>.)
.PP
Hiding a machine\*(Aqs character set without sacrificing too much performance
requires extensive wizardry, not all of which is transparent, and not all of
which yet works.  The project is being actively developed with significant
contributions by IBM of staff and machine time.  The project is called \fBOpen
Source for z/OS\fR.  <https://zopen\-community> brings you to its landing page.
.PP
Basically, files and streams need to have extra information about their
character set.  This can easily require manual intervention to get things to
work.  For background, including what steps you must do to get this to work
on your inputs, see
<https://makingdeveloperslivesbetter.wordpress.com/2022/01/07/is\-z\-os\-ascii\-or\-ebcdic\-yes/>
.PP
You can obtain this Perl port at <https://zopen\-community/perlport>.  At the
moment this is written, one configuration of perl is available: dynamic
loading, unthreaded, non\-debugging.  You can customize it to some extent by
modifying the file \fIbuildenv\fR.  Refer to "Configuring perl" below for hints
on that.  Also, refer to "Known Bugs" below.
.SS "EBCDIC perl"
.IX Subsection "EBCDIC perl"
The rest of this document describes how to build a perl that runs native EBCDIC
on z/OS.
.PP
z/OS Unix System Services has a native shell much like \f(CW\*(C`ksh\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`bash\*(C'\fR, and
Unix commands that are similar to ones in Linux, such as \f(CW\*(C`sed\*(C'\fR.  Available
options for these may differ from the Linux ones.
.PP
There aren\*(Aqt any known 32\-bit z/OS systems in existence anymore, so this
document describes how to build a 64\-bit EBCDIC Perl.
.PP
Most CPAN modules have been written assuming ASCII.  They may or may not work
on EBCDIC.  What we have found is that many do work, either mostly or
completely, but may not appear to pass all their tests.  Often the failures are
in edge cases that may not come up in mainstream uses.  Also any given failure
may not actually be a real failure, but a problem with the test.  This is
because the typical test compares results with expected values which the test\*(Aqs
author often hard\-coded assuming ASCII, so the test is wrong, and the code is
correct.  Also, some failures may not be important for you.  For example, if you
do a plain \f(CW\*(C`sort\*(C'\fR, ASCII will have the digits first, uppercase letters next,
and lowercase last.  EBCDIC sorts the opposite.  If your application really
needs ASCII ordering, the module won\*(Aqt give the proper results; but often you
just need a consistent sorting, and for that purpose the module could work
perfectly well.
.PP
Perl itself is shipped with some CPAN modules included.  Some of these do not
yet work fully on EBCDIC, and therefore testing of them is suppressed.  See
"Bugs only in the EBCDIC mode"
.SS Tools
.IX Subsection "Tools"
The tools required for building perl are available as:
.IP \(bu 4
native commands on z/OS
.Sp
z/OS has versions of many tools that come with the Linux OS.  Some of these
don\*(Aqt have as many options as replacements available in the next group:
.IP \(bu 4
through the zopen community
.Sp
\&\f(CW\*(C`gunzip\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`clang\*(C'\fR for example.  Also you can get \f(CW\*(C`bash\*(C'\fR itself.
.IP \(bu 4
at no cost from IBM
.Sp
<https://www.ibm.com/products/open\-enterprise\-foundation\-zos>.  These
include GNU make, which you will need.
.SS "Building EBCDIC Perl on z/OS"
.IX Subsection "Building EBCDIC Perl on z/OS"
\fIGetting perl source\fR
.IX Subsection "Getting perl source"
.PP
You\*(Aqll first need a tarball.  At the moment this is written, there are no
EBCDIC tarballs published, but it is easy to create one given that you have
access to a system with any relatively modern perl.
.PP
Get an ASCII tarball.  Choose one of the following:
.IP "Official stable release" 4
.IX Item "Official stable release"
Go to <https://www.perl.org/get.html> and choose any one of the "Download
latest Linuxy stable source" buttons.  The name of that tarball will be
something like \fIperl\-V.R.M,tar,gz\fR, where V.R.M is the
version/release/modification of the perl you are downloading.  Do
.Sp
.Vb 2
\&  gunzip perl\-V.R.M.tar.gz
\&  pax \-r \-f perl\-V.R.M.tar
.Ve
.Sp
(or \f(CW\*(C`tar\*(C'\fR instead of \f(CW\*(C`pax\*(C'\fR)
.Sp
This will create a directory named \fIperl\-V.R.M\fR, which you can rename to be
whatever you want.  The instructions below assume you choose \fIperl\fR.
.IP "Development release" 4
.IX Item "Development release"
If instead you want the latest unstable development release, clone Perl:
.Sp
.Vb 1
\&  git clone https://github.com/Perl/perl5.git perl
.Ve
.PP
Either way, after getting the \f(CW\*(C`perl\*(C'\fR directory populated, do
.PP
.Vb 2
\&  cd perl
\&  perl Porting/makerel \-e
.Ve
.PP
This will use whatever perl already is on the system to create an EBCDIC
tarball named \fIperl\-V.R.M.tar.gz\fR.
.PP
Use \f(CW\*(C`scp\*(C'\fR or \f(CW\*(C`sftp\*(C'\fR to copy the tarball to the target z/OS system. Then, from
the directory where it is located, do
.PP
.Vb 4
\&  gunzip perl\-V.R.M.tar.gz
\&  pax \-r \-f perl\-V.R.M.tar
\&  mv perl\-V.R.M perl
\&  cd perl
.Ve
.PP
(You likely can\*(Aqt use \f(CW\*(C`tar\*(C'\fR instead of \f(CW\*(C`pax\*(C'\fR here.)
.PP
\fIConfiguring perl\fR
.IX Subsection "Configuring perl"
.PP
Now it is time to Configure it to your needs.  If you need a dynamically
loading perl (see below), first do:
.PP
.Vb 2
\&  export PATH=$PWD:$PATH
\&  export LIBPATH=$PWD:$LIBPATH
.Ve
.PP
Then run Configure
.PP
.Vb 2
\&  ./Configure \-Dprefix=/path\-to/install/directory \-des \e
\&                                OTHER\-OPTIONS\-AS\-BELOW
.Ve
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-d\*(C'\fR means to use the default configuration values.  This is important, as we
have set those up specifically for z/OS.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-e\*(C'\fR means to continue on to the bitter end, not stopping the process early.
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-s\*(C'\fR means to avoid unnecessary output verbiage.  This parameter is optional.
.PP
If the perl is an unstable development release, add \f(CW\*(C`\-Dusedevel\*(C'\fR as one of the
\&\f(CW\*(C`OTHER\-OPTIONS\-AS\-BELOW\*(C'\fR
.PP
If you think you may want to debug the perl, add \f(CW\*(C`\-DDEBUGGING \-Aoptimize=\-g3
\&\-Aoptimize=\-ggdb3\*(C'\fR.
.PP
To silence some unnecessary compilation warnings, you can add
\&\f(CW\*(C`\-Accflags=\-Wno\-deprecated\*(C'\fR.
.PP
To optimize the resultant perl binary, add \f(CW\*(C`\-Aoptimize=\-O2\*(C'\fR, or \f(CW\*(C`\-O3\*(C'\fR, both
of which have been tested on z/OS 3.1.
.PP
If you will want to use modules from CPAN (like \f(CW\*(C`DBI\*(C'\fR (and \f(CW\*(C`DBD\*(C'\fRs),
\&\f(CW\*(C`JSON::XS\*(C'\fR, and \f(CW\*(C`Text::CSV_XS\*(C'\fR), consider using dynamic loading.  This isn\*(Aqt
necessary if all such modules you might want are written only in Perl, but if
some of them are compiled (meaning they contain XS code), you\*(Aqll either need to
recompile all of perl when you add one, or set it up initially to use dynamic
loading.  To do this, add \f(CW\*(C`\-Dusedl\*(C'\fR to the Configure options.
.PP
When Configure is run using the z/OS native shell, you\*(Aqll see a message like
.PP
.Vb 3
\& (I see you are using the Korn shell.  Some ksh\*(Aqs blow up on Configure,
\& mainly on older exotic systems.  If yours does, try the Bourne shell
\& instead.)
.Ve
.PP
The native shell looks like \f(CW\*(C`ksh\*(C'\fR, and it works fine, so just ignore the
message.
.PP
\&\fIConfigure\fR currently will output errors in finding the header dependencies
various object files have.  This just means that they may be recompiled
unnecessarily.
.PP
\fIBuilding perl\fR
.IX Subsection "Building perl"
.PP
Run GNU make to build Perl
.PP
.Vb 1
\&  make
.Ve
.PP
This will currently likely crash in making the Encode module.  To work around
that, execute the following command
.PP
.Vb 1
\& chtag \-tc IBM\-1047 cpan/Encode/Makefile
.Ve
.PP
and rerun \f(CW\*(C`make\*(C'\fR.  You may have to repeat this a second time.
.PP
\fITesting perl\fR
.IX Subsection "Testing perl"
.PP
.Vb 1
\&  TEST_JOBS=n make test_harness
.Ve
.PP
where \fIn\fR is the number of tests to run in parallel.  On hardware that is
single core, 2 or 3 work well.  A common value (if you aren\*(Aqt crowding out
other users) is to set \fIn\fR to twice the number of cores available, plus 1.
.PP
Some tests currently fail.
.PP
The failing tests in \f(CW\*(C`Test\-Simple\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`Pod\-Html\*(C'\fR, \fIop/signatures\fR, and
\&\fIio/nargv.t\fR are false alarms.
.PP
The failing tests in \fIXS\-APItest/t/utf8_warn00.t\fR show that the generated
warning for a particular very edge case is not exactly as expected and is
somewhat misleading.
.PP
\fIKnown Bugs\fR
.IX Subsection "Known Bugs"
.PP
If zopen is installed on your machine, it is important to get the \f(CW\*(C`PATH\*(C'\fR
variable correct.  When running the zopen port of Perl, its directories should
come first.  When running the EBCDIC perl, the zopen directories should either
not be in the PATH variable, or come last.  For example, \fI/bin/echo\fR needs to
have precedence over the zopen version in this case.
.PP
Bugs in both EBCDIC and ASCII versions
.IX Subsection "Bugs in both EBCDIC and ASCII versions"
.PP
ExtUtils::MakeMaker does not work properly on perls built for dynamic loading.
.PP
Bugs only in the EBCDIC mode
.IX Subsection "Bugs only in the EBCDIC mode"
.PP
Some cpan modules shipped with perl do not yet work fully on EBCDIC, and
therefore testing of them is suppressed.  You can see which by examining
\&\fIt/TEST\fR and searching for EBCDIC.  Almost all of the problems with these are
in the cases where their input is from some other encoding.  These could be
because they communicate with a remote ASCII system, or read from a file
containing some other encoding.  So, for example, Pod::Simple works fine on
files encoded in EBCDIC, but will fail miserably for files encoded in a
Cyrillic encoding.
.PP
Fixes for these are under active development.
.PP
Bugs only in the ASCII mode
.IX Subsection "Bugs only in the ASCII mode"
.PP
Currently, in the zopen port of Perl, there are some issues when using UTF\-8
locales; some of these lead to segfaults.
.PP
\fIInstalling perl\fR
.IX Subsection "Installing perl"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& make install
.Ve
.PP
will install perl into the directory you gave in the command
.PP
.Vb 1
\&  ./Configure \-Dprefix=/path\-to/install/directory ...
.Ve
.SS Optimization
.IX Subsection "Optimization"
Perl is now built using the \f(CW\*(C`clang\*(C'\fR compiler.  Previous releases used
different compilers, and for them, optimization seemed iffy, so we recommended
against doing it.  (And we didn\*(Aqt revisit that advice with newer releases to
see if things had gotten fixed, so that recommendation may have been outdated.)
But with \f(CW\*(C`clang\*(C'\fR it does seem to work, and "Configuring perl" above gives
instructions for enabling it.
.SS "Installing other modules"
.IX Subsection "Installing other modules"
Pure Perl (that is non XS) modules may be installed via the usual:
.PP
.Vb 4
\&    perl Makefile.PL
\&    make
\&    make test
\&    make install
.Ve
.PP
If you built perl with dynamic loading capability, then that would also
be the way to build XS based extensions.  However, if you built perl with
static linking you can still build XS based extensions for z/OS
but you will need to follow the instructions in ExtUtils::MakeMaker for
building statically linked perl binaries.  In the simplest configurations
building a static perl + XS extension boils down to:
.PP
.Vb 6
\&    perl Makefile.PL
\&    make
\&    make perl
\&    make test
\&    make install
\&    make \-f Makefile.aperl inst_perl MAP_TARGET=perl
.Ve
.SS "Running EBCDIC Perl on z/OS"
.IX Subsection "Running EBCDIC Perl on z/OS"
To run the 64\-bit Dynamic Perl environment, update your PATH and LIBPATH
to include the location you installed Perl into, and then run the perl you
installed as perlV.R.M where V/R/M is the Version/Release/Modification level
of the current development level.
